<!--
    GH-904: a tail call through a GOT slot that names an extern with no bytes
    lost its arguments to halt_missing().

    A position-independent tail call loads its target from a slot and jumps
    through it (x86-64 -fno-plt `jmp *ext@GOTPCREL(%rip)`, MIPS
    `lw $t9,%call16(ext)($gp); jr $t9`):

        0x401000 h:       add  edi,1
        0x401003          jmp  qword ptr [0x401010]   ; *0x401010 = 0x402000 (ext)
        0x401018 notfn:   add  edi,2
        0x40101b          jmp  qword ptr [0x401028]   ; *0x401028 = 0x403000
        0x401030 onecase: add  edi,3
        0x401033          jmp  qword ptr [0x401040]   ; *0x401040 = 0x401048
        0x401048          lea  eax,[rdi+5]
        0x40104b          ret

    Jump-table recovery reads the read-only slot and yields a one-entry
    table.  ext is a function symbol with no bytes, as an undefined symbol
    of a relocatable object is, and its prototype is not stated, so
    tailcalljump followed the table into unmapped memory and printed
    `switch(0x402000) { case 0x402000: halt_missing(); }`.

    Stage / sub-stage: P2 "flow-classification".  The P0 assertion
    `option gottailcall on|off` decides whether a one-destination computed
    jump to a function with no bytes is a tail call.  Pass 1 (off)
    reproduces the bug (#1); pass 2 (the shipped on) prints the call with
    the argument the caller set (#2).  Controls hold in both passes: a slot
    naming unmapped memory that is no function (#3), and a slot naming a
    label in the function's own code, a real one-case switch (#4).  The other
    arm of the option, a callee a callee-first decompile-all decompiled
    first, is covered by tests/cli/gottailcall-*.json.
-->
